hey this is presh talaker i received an  email that i wanted to share with you  wolfgang came up with a false proof that  two equals zero  when he showed it to his class no one  could figure out where the mistake was  not even his teacher  so i wanted to share a version of that  proof with you and ask can you figure  out where the mistake is  we start out with two equals one plus  one  one is also equal to the square root of  one  and one is also equal to negative one  times negative one  we now take the square root of each term  the square root of negative one is equal  to the imaginary number i  we have i times i which equals i squared  and i squared is equal to negative one  in the end we have one plus negative one  which equals zero  and all of this is equal to two  so we have proven that two is equal to  zero  clearly this is an absurd conclusion  so where is the mistake in this proof  give it a moment of thought before  watching the rest of the video where i  explain where the mistake comes from  so when wolfgang sent me this email i  immediately spotted the mistake because  it is a common fallacy  the problem comes when you take the  square root of a product of negative  terms  it's okay to take the square root of  negative one times negative one  what you cannot do is then take the  square root of each term  there is something called the product  rule of square roots  if you have the square root of 8 times b  that is equal to the square root of a  times the square root of b but it's  generally only true when both terms are  greater than or equal to zero  it doesn't work in this case when both  terms are negative one you can see  that's the mistake all of the other  steps in the proof are valid  so in math class you sometimes learn  these properties and they come with  these conditions like oh this is only  true both terms are greater than or  equal to zero you can see why these  terms absolutely matter you should  remember the conditions of these  properties because otherwise you can get  to absurd conclusions like in this case  two equals zero  so did you figure it out  thanks for watching this video please  subscribe to my channel i make videos on  math and game theory you can catch one  of my blog mind your decisions which you  can follow on facebook google plus and  patreon you can catch me on social media  at preshtalwalker and if you like this  video please check out my books there  are links in the video description
 

